1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Be2 Na5 8.
Qd3 b5 9. O-O Bb7 10. b3 Rc8 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. Bf3 Nc6 13. Nxc6 dxc6 14. Rfe1 Bd6
15. g3 O-O 16. Rad1 Rfd8 17. Qe2 Be5 18. Nb1 c5 19. Bc3 Bxc3 20. Nxc3 Qe5 21.
Qe3 h6 22. Rd3 $6 (22. Bg2 $142 Ng4 23. Qf3 h5 24. Ne2 Rxd1 25. Rxd1 Nf6 26.
Qc3 Qxc3 27. Nxc3 b4 28. e5 $11) 22... Rxd3 23. Qxd3 c4 24. bxc4 Rxc4 25. Re3
Rd4 26. Qe2 b4 27. Nb1 Qc7 $6 (27... Nxe4 $142) 28. c3 Rc4 29. Qe1 a5 30. h3 e5
31. Kh2 a4 $2 (31... Qc5) 32. cxb4 Rc1 33. Rc3 Rxe1 34. Rxc7 Bxe4 35. Nd2 ({
Rybka prefers} 35. Bxe4 Nxe4 36. Na3 {with white's QNP a threat that stops
black from taking the f-pawn.}) 35... Bxf3 36. Nxf3 Re2 37. Kg1 e4 38. Ne5 Rxa2
39. Ra7 Nd5 40. b5 f5 41. Nc4 Ra1+ 42. Kh2 Rb1 43. b6 Nxb6 44. Nxb6 Rxb6 45.
Rxa4 Rb2 {This ending is covered by Fine pp376-378. See Mikenas,V-Alekhine,A/
Warsaw ol (Men) 1935 (1)/1/2-1/2} 46. Kg1 Rb6 47. Ra5 Rf6 48. h4 {A key move.
Allowing black's h pawn to advance to h4 is dangerous. See Capablanca,J-Yates,
F/Hastings 3031 1930 (2)/1-0} Kf7 49. Kg2 Kg6 50. Ra7 Rf7 51. Ra6+ Kh5 52. Re6
Ra7 53. Re5 Kg4 54. Re6 Kh5 55. Re5 g6 56. Kh3 Ra3 57. Re6 Rf3 58. Kg2 g5 59.
hxg5 hxg5 60. Re7 Kg4 61. Re8 Ra3 62. Re7 Ra5 63. Re8 {Agreed drawn. Although
Rybka is showing a decisive plus to black, even after an hour's thought, it's
actually a book draw!... and has been since move 45.} 1/2-1/2

