1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Nb3 Bb6 6. a4 Qf6 ({Much more
usual now is} 6... a6 7. Nc3 d6 8. Nd5 Ba7 {Rublevsky,S (2686) -Svidler,P 
(2727)/Dortmund Gr-A 2004 (4)/1/2-1/2 e.g.}) 7. Qe2 a6 8. Nc3 Nge7 9. Nd5 Nxd5
10. exd5+ Ne7 11. a5 Ba7 12. h4 h6 13. Ra4 O-O 14. d6 ({A more dangerous line
perhaps is} 14. g4 Nxd5 15. g5 {Totsky,L (2430)-Gross,D (2270)/ Ceske
Budejovice op 1993 (2)/0-1}) 14... Nd5 15. c4 {White was a postal chess IM.
Apparently this had all been played before in one of his postal games, but I
found a better move over the board. Rybka likes 15...Nf4 better, but my move
stops white's attacking threats.} b5 16. cxb5 ({better is} 16. axb6 Nxb6 17.
Ra3 cxd6 18. Na5 d5 19. c5 Nc4 20. Nxc4 dxc4 21. Qxc4 Bb7 {but Black ends up
with a very strong bishop pair.}) 16... axb5 17. Re4 Bb7 18. dxc7 Nc3 19. bxc3
Bxe4 20. Nd4 Rfe8 21. Be3 b4 ({stronger is} 21... Bxd4 22. cxd4 (22. Bxd4 Qf4
23. Be3 Qxc7) 22... Rxa5) 22. Qc4 bxc3 23. Be2 Bxd4 (23... Bxg2 $142 24. Rg1
Bxd4 25. Bxd4 Rxe2+ 26. Qxe2 Qxd4 27. Rxg2 Qb4) 24. Bxd4 Qg6 25. Qxc3 Bxg2 26.
Rh2 Qe4 (26... Qb1+ $142 27. Kd2 Qa2+ 28. Kc1 Qxe2) 27. Be3 d5 28. Bd3 Qg4 29.
Kd2 Bf3 30. Bb5 Rec8 31. Bb6 Qf4+ 32. Qe3 Qxh2 33. Qxf3 Qd6 (33... Rxc7) 34.
Bf1 d4 35. Bh3 Qb4+ 0-1

