1. e4 e5 2. f4 Qh4+ $5 {Played to get him out of the book and induce him to
his usual habit of taking too long on the opening.} 3. g3 Qe7 4. Nc3 exf4 5.
Bc4 (5. d4 fxg3 6. hxg3 d5 7. Bf4 {has been played more often, with wins for
white and black and hardly any draws! e.g. Gallagher,J (2500)-Berezovsky,I/
Bern op 1993 (8)/1-0}) 5... fxg3 6. Nf3 {Around here and the next few moves,
white should probably have taken on g3 and, if not, black should have taken on
h2.} (6. hxg3 c6 {Morawietz,M-Busch,A/PF-op Bellheim 1994/1-0}) 6... c6 7. O-O
d6 8. d4 Bh3 9. Re1 Bg4 10. Bg5 Qc7 11. hxg3 Nd7 12. e5 d5 13. Nxd5 $5 {
White needs to do something. Otherwise black will just consolidate a pawn up.
But by now white had used up an hour and forty five minutes to black's 40
minutes, so had 15 minutes left for the next 35 moves!} cxd5 14. Bxd5 Nb6 15.
Bb3 h6 16. Bf4 O-O-O 17. Re3 g5 $2 (17... Bxf3) ({Houdini 3 Pro thinks that
best is} 17... Nd5) 18. Rc3 Bc5 19. Be3 Qc6 20. Rxc5 Qxc5 21. dxc5 Rxd1+ 22.
Rxd1 Nd7 23. Rf1 Ne7 24. Bd4 {Now white has a sizeable advantage, but he had
less than two minutes on the clock.} Be6 25. Nd2 Nc6 26. c3 Ndxe5 27. Bxe6+
fxe6 28. Re1 $2 (28. Nc4 Nxd4 29. Nxe5 Nf5 30. g4 Ne3 31. Rf6 $16) 28... Rd8
29. Bxe5 Rxd2 30. Bg7 Rxb2 31. Rxe6 h5 $2 ({Eliminating all the king's side
pawns ensures a draw, but makes it easier for white to find moves in severe
time trouble.} 31... Rxa2 32. Bxh6 a5 33. Bxg5 Rc2 34. Bf4 Rxc3 35. Kf2 Rxc5
$17) 32. a4 h4 33. gxh4 gxh4 34. Rh6 Kd7 35. Rxh4 Ne7 $2 {makes white's moves
easier to find.} 36. Rb4 Rxb4 37. cxb4 Kc6 38. Bd4 a6 39. Kf2 Nd5 40. b5+ axb5
41. axb5+ Kxb5 42. Ke2 Kc4 43. Bg1 Nc3+ 44. Kd2 Ne4+ 45. Kc2 Nxc5 46. Kb2 Nd3+
47. Kc2 b5 48. Be3 (48. Be3 {Since white had survived to the time control we
agreed a draw.} b4 49. Bd2 b3+ 50. Kb1 {and black can't make progress.})
1/2-1/2

