1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Be2 O-O 6. O-O Nc6 7. Be3 Bg4 8. d5
Nb8 9. Nd2 Bxe2 10. Qxe2 c6 11. Rad1 b5 12. dxc6 (12. a3 $142 a6 13. dxc6 Nxc6
14. Nd5 Rc8 15. c3 {with a slight advantage}) 12... b4 13. Nd5 Nxc6 14. Nxf6+
Bxf6 15. Nc4 Qc8 16. b3 Qa6 17. Bh6 Rfc8 18. f4 Bc3 19. Qf2 Qxa2 20. Rd3 ({
Houdini prefers} 20. e5 {after which it thinks black needs to jettison two
pieces for a rook with} d5 21. Rxd5 Bd4 22. Rxd4 Nxd4 23. Qxd4 Qxc2 24. Qd7 Re8
) 20... f6 21. e5 dxe5 $2 22. Rxc3 $6 ({Houdini Pro finds} 22. Qc5 {immediately
} Kh8 23. Qd5 Bd4+ 24. Kh1 Nd8 25. fxe5 $18) 22... bxc3 23. fxe5 f5 ({This is
actually the losing move! Although even Houdini Pro assesses it the position
as equal. If black had seen the coming combination, he could have played} 23...
Nxe5 24. Nxe5 Qa6 $14 25. Nc4 {limiting white to a small advantage.}) 24. Qc5
Qxc2 (24... Qa6 {has some beautiful "unheard melodies". (24...Rc7 is a bit
tougher, but it still loses.)} 25. Qd5+ Kh8 26. Qf7 Rg8 27. Nd6 $1 {threat 28.
Qf6+ and 29.Nf7 mate.} Qb6+ 28. Kh1 Qc7 {Now the black queen is indirectly
defending f7, so what can white do?} 29. Rf3 $1 Nxe5 ({If} 29... Rac8 30. Rxc3
Nxe5 31. Rxc7 Rxc7 32. Qe6 exd6 33. Qxd6 {wins, but not so prettily}) ({If} 
29... Qd7 30. Rh3 f4 31. Bf8 Qxh3 32. Qf6+ exf6 33. Nf7# {is another pretty
mate!}) 30. Rh3 Qxd6 31. Qxh7+ Kxh7 32. Bf8#) 25. Qd5+ Kh8 26. Qf7 Rg8 27. Nd6
$1 Raf8 28. Bg7+ 1-0

