1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. O-O d6 6. Nc3 Nc6 7. Rb1 a5 8. a3
Bf5 ({Much more usual is} 8... e5 9. d3 h6 10. b4 axb4 11. axb4 Be6 {with
nearly 1000 games on the database and a near 50% overall score for black,
including some good wins, e.g. Polugaevsky,L-Kortschnoj,V/URS Soviet Union
1975/0-1}) 9. d3 Qd7 10. b4 axb4 11. axb4 Bh3 12. b5 Nd8 13. e3 (13. Bb2 Bxg2
14. Kxg2 e5 {Minasian,A (2555)-Fedorov,A (2604)/Dubai op 5th 2003 (5)/ 1-0})
13... Bxg2 14. Kxg2 e5 15. Nd2 Ne6 16. Kg1 $15 Nh5 17. Qf3 $2 Nc5 $2 (17... e4
$1 18. Ndxe4 ({or} 18. Ncxe4 f5) 18... f5 $17 {wins a piece}) 18. Nb3 Nxb3 19.
Rxb3 b6 20. Ra3 Rxa3 21. Bxa3 f5 {I had already envisaged the idea of d6-d5
here} 22. Qc6 Qxc6 23. bxc6 Ra8 24. Ra1 e4 25. d4 Nf6 26. Nb5 Ne8 27. Bb2 Rxa1+
28. Bxa1 Kf7 29. Kf1 d5 30. cxd5 Bf8 31. Bc3 Bd6 32. Nxd6+ $2 (32. Be1) 32...
cxd6 $1 33. Bb4 ({As recommended by Rybka. If} 33. Kg2 Nc7 34. Kh3 Nxd5 35. Kh4
h6 36. g4 g5+ 37. Kg3 f4+ 38. exf4 gxf4+ 39. Kg2 Ke6 $17 {It takes a while for
Rybka (and my opponent) to recognise the strength of black's position now})
33... Ke7 34. Ke2 Nc7 35. Kd2 Nxd5 36. Ba3 Ke6 37. Kc2 Nc7 38. Kb3 Kd5 39. Bb4
Kxc6 40. Ba3 Ne6 {and white resigned on adjudication} 0-1

