{At this stage Pritchett and I were in the joint lead on 4/4 with two rounds
to go.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. g4 a6 7. g5 Nfd7
8. Be3 b5 9. a3 Nb6 10. h4 N8d7 11. h5 {I hadn't prepared this at all, but
Andrew Law (who was a Scheveningen expert) suggested this as the most
dangerous line for white.} Bb7 (11... Ne5 12. f4 Nec4 13. Bc1 Qc7 14. Qd3 {
Bolacky,J (2267)-Didi,J (2131)/ Orlova op 2005 (7)/1-0}) 12. Rh3 $146 {A
novelty found over the board!Craig Pritchett's recently published book on this
opening only gave 12.Rg1. The database gives a game from December 1977. The
point is that black can't now play g6} ({Another novelty, played a few years
later, was} 12. g6 hxg6 13. hxg6 Rxh1 14. gxf7+ Kxf7 15. Qf3+ Qf6 (15... Nf6
16. Qxh1 {Svidler,P (2650) -Nepomniashchy,M (2480)/St Petersburg-ch 1996 (3)/
1-0}) 16. Qxh1 Nc5 $14 (16... Ne5 17. Nxe6 {Adams,M (2600)-Hansen,C (2565)/
Hoogovens Wijk aan Zee 1991 (7)/ 1-0}) 17. O-O-O {Adams,M (2600)-Hansen,C 
(2565)/Hoogovens Wijk aan Zee 1991 (7) /1-0}) 12... Qe7 (12... Be7 13. g6 {
Kotronias,V (2500)-Topalov,V (2495)/ Balkaniad Kavala 1990 (4)/1-0}) (12... Ne5
{De Firmian,N (2625)-Winslow,E (2475)/CalChess Masters 1983 (8)/1-0}) (12...
Nc5 {Emerson v Nicholson J. Hereford Open 1978}) (12... d5 13. g6 e5 14. Nf5
hxg6 15. hxg6 Rxh3 16. gxf7+ Kxf7 17. Bxh3 $16 {Yakovich,Y (2440)-Espig,L 
(2395)/Leipzig DSV 1986 (5)/1-0}) 13. g6 O-O-O $2 14. gxf7 Qxf7 15. Qg4 $6 (15.
Rf3 $142 $5) 15... Re8 16. Rf3 Ne5 17. Rxf7 Nxg4 18. Bh3 (18. Nxe6 $142 $1)
18... Nxe3 19. fxe3 Kb8 20. Bxe6 g6 21. Nd5 ({Here I start to let the
advantage slip away} 21. Ke2 $142 Nc4 22. Bxc4 bxc4 23. Raf1 $18) 21... Nxd5
22. exd5 Re7 23. Ke2 Rxf7 24. Bxf7 Bg7 25. Rg1 (25. Kd3 $142 gxh5 26. Rh1 Rf8
27. Be6 $16) 25... Bxd4 26. exd4 gxh5 27. Rg8+ $2 (27. Be6 $142 h4 28. Rg4 h3
29. Rh4 h5 30. Rxh3 $16) 27... Rxg8 28. Bxg8 Kc7 29. Kf3 h4 30. Kg4 Bc8+ 31.
Kxh4 Bf5 32. c3 Be4 33. Kg5 a5 34. Kh6 a4 ({Houdini thinks that black should
play} 34... Kd8 35. Bxh7 Bxd5 36. Bd3 Bc6 37. Kg6 Ke7 {to keep the white king
at bay}) 35. Bxh7 Bxd5 36. Kg5 $2 ({Rybka assesses this as +1.36 for white,
but in reality it should be a draw. The alternative to the text is} 36. Bd3 {
The plan is first to tie black's king or bishop to the defence of the pawn on
b5 and then try to infiltrate the white king round the back of black's
position.}) 36... Bc4 37. Kf6 Be2 38. Ke7 Bc4 39. Bf5 Be2 40. Be6 Bd3 41. b3 (
41. Bd5 {is given +- by Rybka, but after} Be2 42. Kf6 Kd7 43. Kf5 Ke7 44. Kf4
Bd3 45. Ke3 Bf1 46. Kd2 Bc4 {white's king can't get to support b2-b3}) 41...
axb3 42. Bxb3 Be2 43. a4 ({Houdini gets all excited about} 43. Bf7 Bd3 44. Be8
Be2 {but then can't get anywhere}) (43. d5 Bf3 44. Ba2 Be2 45. a4 {fails by a
tempo} bxa4 46. c4 Bg4 47. c5 dxc5 48. d6+ Kc6 49. Be6 Bxe6 50. Kxe6 a3 51. d7
a2 52. d8=Q a1=Q) 43... bxa4 44. Bxa4 Bc4 45. Bd7 Bd5 $4 ({A blunder in time
trouble, coming up to the control on move 48.} 45... Ba2 46. Be6 d5 47. Bd7 (
47. Bf7 Kc6 48. Ke6 Bb3 49. Be8+ Kc7 50. Ke5 Bc4 51. Ba4 Ba2 $11) 47... Bb3 48.
Ke6 Bc4 49. Ba4 Ba2 50. Bb5 Kb6 51. Bd3 Kc6 {draws}) 46. Be6 $18 Bc6 47. c4 Be4
48. d5 (48. d5 Bf3 49. c5 dxc5 50. d6+ {and the pawn queens}) 1-0

