1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. c4 Nb6 5. exd6 cxd6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Be3 Bg7 8. h3
O-O 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. d5 $6 ({More normal is} 10. Be2 Bf5 11. O-O d5 12. c5 Nc4
13. Bxc4 dxc4 14. Qa4 Bd3 {Osnos,V-Suetin,A/URS-ch33 Tallinn 1965 (7)/1/2-1/2
e.g.}) 10... Ne5 ({Better would be} 10... Na5 11. Bd4 Bxd4 12. Qxd4 e5 13. dxe6
Bxe6 14. b3 {Schweber,S (2395)-Piza Cortizo,D/Buenos Aires BCA op 1988/0-1} d5
15. c5 Nc6 $15 {I was worried about my queen's knight getting trapped on the
edge of the board.}) 11. Nxe5 Bxe5 12. Bd4 Nd7 13. Be2 Bxd4 14. Qxd4 Qb6 15.
Qxb6 Nxb6 16. Kd2 Bd7 17. Rhe1 Rac8 18. b3 Rfd8 $6 ({The start of an incorrect
plan. I'd missed white's 23rd move. Better is passive defence with} 18... a6
19. Bg4 f5 20. Bf3 Rf7 {although white still retains some advantage.}) 19. Bg4
Bxg4 20. hxg4 e6 21. dxe6 fxe6 22. Rxe6 d5 23. c5 $1 $16 Rxc5 24. Re7 Rdc8 25.
Rc1 R8c7 26. Rxc7 Rxc7 27. Re1 d4 28. Nb5 Rf7 29. Re8+ Kg7 30. f3 Nd5 31. Nxd4
Rd7 32. Rd8 $1 Rxd8 33. Ne6+ Kf6 34. Nxd8 b6 35. a4 a5 36. g3 $2 (36. Nc6 Ke6
37. g3 Kd6 38. Nd4 {preserves the advantage.}) 36... Nb4 $1 {trapping white's
knight.} 37. f4 Ke7 38. Nb7 Kd7 {Agreed drawn on adjournment.} (38... Kd7 {
White can play} 39. f5 {and although he loses his knight, he can create a
passed pawn on the queen's side tying down black's king for a draw.}) 1/2-1/2

