1. e4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e5 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Nd4 5. O-O Bc5 6. Nxe5 O-O 7. Be2 d5 8.
exd5 Re8 9. Nc4 Rxe2 $5 ({Rybka prefers} 9... Nxd5 10. Bf3 c6 {The text is an
attempt to avoid a sterile draw}) 10. Nxe2 ({Rybka thinks that} 10. Ne3 $1 Rxe3
11. dxe3 Nf5 12. e4 Ne7 13. Qe2 {gives white a slight advantage because of the
central pawns}) 10... Bg4 11. Re1 Nxd5 $2 ({Much stronger is} 11... Ne4 $1) 12.
c3 $2 ({After} 12. h3 Qh4 $1 13. hxg4 Nf3+ $1 14. gxf3 {Black has a draw by
perpetual check or he can try for more with} Bxf2+ 15. Kf1 Qh2 16. Ne3 Nxe3+
17. dxe3 Bh4 18. Ng3 Qh3+ 19. Kg1 Qxg3+ 20. Kh1 Qh3+ 21. Kg1 Rd8 22. Bd2 Bxe1
23. Qxe1 Qxf3 {which Rybka assesses as slightly better for black because of
the continuing threats against the white king}) 12... Nxe2+ 13. Rxe2 Nf4 14.
Qc2 Nxe2+ 15. Kf1 Qh4 16. d4 Qxh2 17. f3 Bxf3 (17... Bxf3 {Black mates in 10
at most, e.g.} 18. gxf3 Ng3+ 19. Ke1 Re8+ 20. Be3 Qxc2 21. Nd2 Rxe3+ 22. Kf2
Qxd2+ 23. Kxg3 Bd6+ 24. Kh4 Qh2+ 25. Kg4 Qf4+ 26. Kh5 g6#) 0-1

