1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Qc7 7. Qg4 f6 8. Bb5+
Kf8 9. Nf3 $1 c4 ({This line was a recommendation in a book on the French by
Moles, but I had an improvement for white, first seen in my game against
Andrew Law from 1975} 9... a6 10. Be2 (10. Bd3 cxd4 11. O-O dxc3 12. a4 f5 13.
Ba3+ Ne7 14. Qh5 {Kindermann,S (2530)-Mohr,S (2485)/ Altensteig 1989/1-0})
10... cxd4 11. O-O dxc3 12. a4) 10. a4 Qf7 (10... a6 11. Ba3+ Ne7 ({If} 11...
Kf7 {as played in 1975 by Andrew Law (with a severe head cold - he really
shouldn't have been playing)} 12. Ng5+ fxg5 13. Qh5+ g6 14. Qf3+ Nf6 15. Qxf6+
Kg8 16. Qf8#) 12. Be8 $1 Kxe8 13. Qxg7 Rf8 (13... Rg8 14. Qxh7 f5 15. Bd6 Qd7
16. h4 Nbc6 17. Ng5 $16) 14. exf6 Rf7 15. Qh8+ Rf8 16. Qxh7 {Svensson,B (2508)
-Ivarsson,S (2297)/SWE-chT 0708 2007 (4)/1-0} ({the stem game on my database
has a game from December 1978 in which they agreed a draw (!) after} 16. Qg7 {
Hayward,K (2106)-Carter,D (2072)/Portland op 1978/1/2-1/2})) 11. Ba3+ Ne7 12.
Qf4 f5 13. Qh4 (13. Ng5 $142 Qg6 14. Qh4 Qh6 15. Nxh7+ Kg8 16. Qxh6 gxh6 17.
Nf6+ Kf7 18. a5 $16) 13... Nbc6 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Ng5 Qe8 16. Nxh7+ Kg8 17.
Qxe7 Qxe7 18. Bxe7 Kxh7 19. Ke2 Bd7 20. Bd6 {Black resigned - somewhat
prematurely. With opposite coloured bishops, there are chances of a draw,
although white's control of the a-file gives him what should be a winning
advantage.} 1-0

