{Before the start of the game, somebody let slip that they had an opening
innovation prepared against me. What the hell- let's see what they have
prepared!} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4
b5 8. e5 dxe5 9. fxe5 Qc7 10. Qe2 Nfd7 11. O-O-O Bb7 12. Qg4 Qxe5 13. Bxb5 axb5
14. Rhe1 h5 15. Qh4 Qc5 16. Qg3 h4 ({I understood at the time that this was a
novelty black had prepared for our game, although the Big Database shows an
earlier game from 1973. Theory is} 16... Bc6 {Black's novelty stops white's
intended h4 protecting his bishop, but it allows white to gang up again on the
vulnerable e6 square. For analysis after 16...Bc6, see Emerson,R-Bowmer,K 
(1994)/Charlton Open 1977 (2)/1-0}) 17. Qg4 Ra6 $146 ({This is the real
novelty! It's stronger than} 17... Nf6 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Qxe6+ Be7 20. Bxf6
gxf6 {Helman,A (2256)-Parr,D (2152)/Charlton op 1973 (4)/1/2-1/2} 21. Rd6 $1
Qg5+ 22. Kb1 Rh7 23. Ne4 Bxe4 24. Qxe4 $16) 18. Ncxb5 Bd5 19. Qf4 ({Rybka
suggests} 19. Qf5 f6 20. Qg6+ Kd8 21. Be3 {but Houdini think's it's rubbish!
Houdini thinks my move is best}) ({Stockfish finds} 19. Rd3 f6 20. Rc3 Qb6 21.
Nc7+ Kf7 22. Nxd5 exd5 23. Rc6 Nxc6 24. Qe6+ Kg6 25. Qf5+ Kf7 26. Qe6+ {
with a draw by repetition.}) 19... Bd6 $2 ({Both Houdini and Rybka like} 19...
f6 $1 20. Nc7+ Kf7 21. Nxa6 Nxa6) 20. Nxd6+ Qxd6 ({If} 20... Rxd6 21. Be7 $1
Kxe7 22. Nf5+ Kd8 (22... Kf8 23. Qg5 $1) 23. Nxd6 Rf8 24. c4 g5 25. Qxg5+ Kc7
26. Kb1 Qxd6 27. cxd5 $18) 21. Qxd6 Rxd6 22. Nf5 Ra6 23. Rxd5 Kf8 (23... f6
$142 24. Rd6 (24. Bd2 O-O 25. Ne7+ Kf7 26. Rd4 Re8 27. Bb4 Rxe7 $11) 24... O-O
25. Rxa6 Nxa6 26. Bxf6 Rxf6 27. Nxh4 $11) 24. Nd6 Nf6 $2 25. Rc5 Rh5 26. Ne4
Nbd7 27. Rc8+ Ke7 28. Rd1 $2 ({I misanalysed} 28. Rc7 {but it wins} Kf8 (28...
Kd8 29. Rxd7+ Kxd7 30. Bxf6 Rxa2 31. Kb1 $18) 29. a3 Kg8 30. Bxf6 Nxf6 31. Rc8+
Kh7 32. Nxf6+ gxf6 33. Rc3 Kg7 34. b4 $18) 28... Rxa2 29. Kb1 Ra5 30. Nxf6 (30.
Bxf6+ $142 gxf6 31. c4) 30... gxf6 31. Rc7 Rd5 ({If} 31... Rhxg5 32. Rdxd7+ Ke8
33. Rxf7 $16 {The next 9 moves were in time shortage for both of us.}) 32. Rxd5
exd5 33. Bf4 Ke6 34. g3 hxg3 35. hxg3 Ne5 36. Rb7 Ng6 37. Rb6+ Kf5 38. Bc7 Ne5
39. Rd6 Rh1+ 40. Ka2 Rd1 41. Bd8 Ng4 (41... d4 42. Rxf6+ Ke4 {still leaves
white with only a slight advantage.}) 42. b4 Ke5 43. Bc7 Rd4 44. b5 Rb4 (44...
Ke4 45. Kb3 Rd1 46. Kb2 Rd4 47. Ba5 Ne3 48. Rxf6 Nc4+ 49. Kc3) 45. c3 $18 Ra4+
46. Kb3 Ra7 47. b6 Rb7 48. Ka4 Ne3 49. Kb5 Nc4 (49... Nc4 {and black resigned
in view of} 50. Rc6+ Kf5 51. Ka6) 1-0

